  "answer": "**Final status: passed.** The second verification run (`20261021T131158Z-a23f2a`) passed all three configured stages: build, unit and component. It took one fix cycle.\n\n**What failed:** The first run (`20261021T122110Z-836fe4`) failed at the `unit` stage with exit code 2, so the `component` stage was skipped. According to the fixer, 38 test methods failed on both net9.0 and net10.0. They were in `ResultMatcherTests` and `SemanticMatcherTests`, and each threw `ArgumentException: Unknown semantic matcher 'isuuid'\u2026`, always with the matcher name in lowercase.\n\n**Root cause:** `ParseSpec` in `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs` converted matcher names to lowercase with `ToLowerInvariant()`. The built-in matchers are registered in camelCase (`isUuid`, `greaterThan`, \u2026), and both the built-in and custom matcher lookups are case-sensitive. So no semantic matcher could ever be found.\n\n**What was changed:** The fixer removed the two `ToLowerInvariant()` calls in `ParseSpec`, at `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs:85` and `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs:91`. Matcher names are now used exactly as written. `ValidateSpecs` uses the same parser, so it is fixed too. No test files were touched.\n\n- **Case-sensitivity choice:** If the lowercasing was meant to make matcher names case-insensitive, the alternative fix is a case-insensitive lookup. No existing test expects that, so names now have to match exactly.\n- **Not checked by me:** As you asked, I didn't open the logs or the diff. The failing tests and the root cause above come from the fixer's report; the passing result comes from the verifier.\n\nNothing was committed.",
